{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And his master shall bring him.</b> The word Elohim is like 'those who uphold the judgment of Elohim in the land' [Job 36:6], and the custom was that they would sit at the city gate that has doors and a bar, and this is the meaning of 'unto the door' or 'unto the doorpost' — that this would be done before those who sit at the gate." "<b>And he shall serve him forever.</b> We know that the word לעולם in the sacred language denotes a span of time, like 'it was already of old' (Ecclesiastes 1:10) — times; and 'abide there forever' (I Samuel 1:22) — until a time when he would be grown. So too 'and he shall serve him לעולם' — for the span of his Jubile cycle, since no fixed period of Israel is longer than it, and the release to freedom is as though the עולם begins anew. Or its meaning may be that he returns to his original state, in which he is free."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:36:42.798586374Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}